<section xml:id="exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <p>
    <alert>NOTE:</alert> You are not expected to have solved all of these exercises
    in class. Their sole purpose is to start having you think about the material
    while I'm there, so we can talk about it together.
  </p>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following system of linear equations in variables
        <m>x_{1}, x_{2}, y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}, y_{4}</m>:
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>x_{1} + y_{1} \amp = 1</mrow>
          <mrow>-x_{1} + y_{2} \amp = 0</mrow>
          <mrow>x_{2} + y_{3} \amp = 1</mrow>
          <mrow>-x_{2} + y_{4} \amp = 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write down the augmented matrix.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The augmented matrix is
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Put the augmented matrix in reduced row echelon form.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To put it in reduced row echelon form, you first look for the left most
          column with nonzero entry. That is your first pivot column. Pick a
          favorite row whose leading entry is in that column. I pick the first
          row. Then rescale it so the leading entry is <m>1</m>. That is already
          the case, so I don't do anything. Then for every other row with same
          leading entry, replace it with the row plus a multiple of the first row
          that cancels out that entry. Here the only row other than the first with
          the leading entry in the first column is the second row, so I replace
          the second row with it plus the first row to get the following:
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Now there is a unique row, the first row, with leading entry in the first
          column, and that entry is equal to <m>1</m>. That is what we want, so now
          we look at all rows with next leftmost leading entry. Here those are rows
          <m>3</m> and <m>4</m>. I pick my favorite. Here I choose row <m>3</m>.
          You rescale it so its leading entry. Here that is already the case, so
          we're golden. Now we take any other row with leading entry in the same
          column. The only other row like that is row 4. We want to cancel out the
          entry in that column. To do that we replace it with it plus the suitable
          scaling of our favorite row, row 3. Here we replace row 4 with row 4 plus
          row 3.
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Now the second column has a unique nonzero entry equal to <m>1</m>. We
          move that row up to the top using interchange directly below the row with
          the previous pivot element. This yields the following matrix:
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Now again we look at the next column with earliest leading entry among
          the remaining rows. That is the third column. We pick our favorite row
          with leading entry in that column. That is row <m>3</m>. Then we replace
          any other row with a nonzero entry in that column by adding a scalar
          multiple of row <m>3</m> to cancel it out. In this case, that row would
          be row <m>1</m>. And to cancel out the third entry in row <m>1</m>, I
          subtract row <m>3</m>. This yields the following:
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Now there is a unique nonzero entry in the third column. It is equal to
          <m>1</m>, and it is in the row at the top among the remaining rows to
          look at. That means we move on to looking at the last row. I look at the
          leading entry. It is in column <m>5</m>. It is equal to <m>1</m> already,
          so we don't need to scale it. There is <m>1</m> other row with a nonzero
          entry in that column, which is row <m>2</m>. We cancel it out with
          replacement:
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          After all that labor, the resulting matrix is in reduced row echelon
          form.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>What is the dimension of the solution set?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          There are four pivot columns, columns <m>1, 2, 3,</m> and <m>5</m>
          corresponding to variables <m>x_{1}, x_{2}, y_{1}, y_{3}</m>. Those are
          our dependent variables. There are three non-pivot columns. Two
          correspond to independent variables <m>y_{2}</m> and <m>y_{4}</m>. The
          final column corresponds to constants. Since there are two independent
          variables, the solution set is two dimensional. The resulting system of
          equations is
          <md number="no">
            <mrow>x_{1} - y_{2} \amp = 0</mrow>
            <mrow>x_{2} - y_{4} \amp = 0</mrow>
            <mrow>y_{1} + y_{2} \amp = 1</mrow>
            <mrow>y_{3} + y_{4} \amp = 1</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Give an explicit parametrization of the solution set.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Since <m>y_{2}</m> and <m>y_{4}</m> are independent variables, to
          parametrize the solution set, we solve in terms of <m>y_{2}</m> and
          <m>y_{4}</m>. Then we get
          <md number="no">
            <mrow>x_{1} \amp = y_{2}</mrow>
            <mrow>x_{2} \amp = y_{4}</mrow>
            <mrow>y_{1} \amp = 1-y_{2}</mrow>
            <mrow>y_{3} \amp = 1-y_{4}</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus, the plane we get if we let <m>s = y_{2}</m> and
          <m>t = y_{4}</m> is
          <me>f(s,t) = (s,t,1-s,s,1-t,t).</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following matrix:
        <me>
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
          \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Put the matrix in reduced row echelon form.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix is very close. We just need to do some interchanging. Reorder
          so that the last row comes first, then the third row comes second, the
          first row comes third, and the second row comes last. This yields the
          following matrix:
          <me>
            \begin{pmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{pmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>The resulting matrix is in reduced row echelon form.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose this is the augmented matrix of a linear system. Will that
          linear system be consistent? Explain why or why not.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Suppose it is the augmented matrix of a linear system. The last column
          is not a pivot column, so the linear system would be consistent.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following matrix:
        <me>
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose this is the coefficient matrix of a linear system. For which
        right hand sides is it consistent?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The augmented matrix for the system would look like:
        <me>
          \begin{pmatrix}
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp a \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp b \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp c
          \end{pmatrix}
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        I could do like last time in put it in reduced row echelon form. Instead
        if I write out the equations I get,
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>x_{3} + 3x_{4} \amp = a</mrow>
          <mrow>x_{2} + 2x_{4} \amp = b</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp = c</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Then <m>c = 0</m>, but <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> can be any thing. In
        particular, <m>(0,b,a,0)</m> is always a solution for any choice of
        <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, when <m>c = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</section>
